    "evidence_text": "All statistical tests were two-sided.   \n\n\nThe primary endpoint was the number of CIN grade 3 or worse detected.\n\nAnalysis was done by intention to screen.\n\nEarly detection of high-grade cervical legions caused by HPV16 was a major component of this benefit.\n\nRelative risks (RRs) were stratified by HPV comparison group.\n\nThe magnitude of association varied by duration of persistence and testing interval.\n\nThese findings validate HPV persistence as a clinical marker and endpoint.\n\nWhether HPV DNA testing alone is useful in primary screening remains to be determined.   \n\n\nRESULTS The estimated prevalence of CIN 3 or higher was 3.2%.\n\nCONCLUSIONS Testing for HPV has higher sensitivity but lower specificity than thin-layer Pap screening.\n\nDESIGN Randomised trial.   \n\n\nWe assessed the efficacy of cervical-cancer screening policies that are based on HPV testing.   \n\n\nWe also retrieved the histological diagnoses from screening done elsewhere.\n\nHowever, in younger women, HPV screening leads to over-diagnosis of regressive CIN2.   \n\n\nEvidence regarding human papillomavirus testing in secondary prevention of cervical cancer.\n\nHuman papillomavirus and Papanicolaou tests to screen for cervical cancer.",
